---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import PlayableGame from "../../../../components/ui/PlayableGame.astro";

import JunkyardPoster from "../../../../assets/courses/game-design/img/junkyard-poster.png";
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/game-design/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Play Junkyard</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <p>
          <b class="text-sn-theme">This is what you are building.</b> Stack of
          crates, one heavy stone ball, and a mouse. It took sixteen crates and
          eight lines of code, and you will have built all of it by the end of
          Activity 1.5.
        </p>

        <PlayableGame
          slug="junkyard"
          title="Junkyard"
          poster={JunkyardPoster}
        />

        <p class="text-secondary">
          <b>Controls:</b> click anywhere to launch the ball. That is the whole
          control scheme.
        </p>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="whats-in-here">
          <h2>What's In Here</h2>

          <p>
            Nothing in this game is decoration. Every part of it is one of the
            five activities in this section.
          </p>

          <table class="table table-dark table-striped">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>What you can see</th>
                <th>Where you build it</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>Anything at all on screen</td>
                <td>Activity 1.1</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Crates sitting where you put them, at the size you chose</td>
                <td>Activity 1.2</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Crates that fall, land, and lean on each other</td>
                <td>Activity 1.3</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Sixteen crates that are all the same crate</td>
                <td>Activity 1.4</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>A ball that answers your mouse and wrecks the stack</td>
                <td>Activity 1.5</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>

          <p>
            Notice what the game is <i>not</i> doing. Nothing keeps score.
            Nothing can be won or lost. No crate knows it was hit. The engine is
            running gravity and collisions, and everything you find satisfying
            about knocking the stack over comes out of that for free.
          </p>

          <p>
            That will not be true of the next game you build. Worth remembering
            when you get there.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="before-you-build">
          <h2>Before You Build</h2>

          <p>
            Play it for two minutes, then be ready to answer these out loud. No
            writing, nothing to submit.
          </p>

          <ol>
            <li>
              Where did you aim the second time, and why was it different from
              the first?
            </li>
            <li>
              This game has no score and no way to win. Why is it still worth
              two minutes?
            </li>
            <li>
              Name <b>one thing</b> you would add. Keep it to one, and keep it
              small enough to build in a day.
            </li>
          </ol>

          <p class="text-secondary">
            Hold onto your answer to the third one. Some of them are already
            built as <b>DLC</b> for this game, and the rest are fair game once
            you can write the code.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
